"The Quran is introduced as a luminous book that clarifies all things. Tilawah (recitation) means to follow, to pursue, and specifically refers to reciting the books revealed by Allah or carefully observing the commandments and prohibitions within them. Here, tilawah is a metaphor for reciting and revealing through Gabriel (Jibril). When Pharaoh learned that a boy would be born from the Children of Israel who would overthrow his kingdom, he began killing the children.\n\nPharaoh was slaughtering innocent boys, seeking to leave the girls without men so he could exploit them as he wished. Allah made known to Pharaoh and Haman that He would bring upon them the very thing they feared, and that their kingdom would come to an end on account of the Children of Israel. He promised to make them leaders and inheritors—that is, to make the oppressed people inheritors of the eastern and western lands that He had filled with blessings.\n\nMoses's mother was inspired through revelation (wahy). It has been stated that this revelation was not the revelation of prophethood, but rather inspiration or a dream. Allah gave her the glad tidings that He would return Moses to her and make him one of the messengers. This inspiration occurred after Moses was born and had been nursed for a time. The word Nile (Nil) conveys the sense of 'negligence' (taqsir), not 'error' (khata'). This event served as an occasion for testing Pharaoh's household.\n\nThe heart of Moses's mother became utterly empty in the morning, filled with distress at receiving no news of Moses. She had almost disclosed his identity, but Allah strengthened her heart. She told Moses's sister to follow him. She watched him from afar, and Pharaoh's household did not realize that she was his sister. Referring to her as 'his sister' here is more eloquent in emphasizing affection and compassion.\n\nWhen Moses reached the age of full maturity, Allah granted him wisdom and knowledge. One day, upon entering the city, he found two men fighting, one from his own people and the other from his enemies. When the one from his people asked for help, Moses struck the enemy with a fist, causing his death, and said, 'This is of Satan's doing.' He then repented and sought Allah's forgiveness. The next day, the same man asking for help again prompted Moses to say, 'Indeed, you are clearly a troublemaker!' The people of the city plotted to kill Moses; a man warned him, and Moses departed from there, fearful and watchful.\n\nHeading toward Midian (Madyan), Moses said, 'I hope my Lord will guide me to the right path.' When he arrived at the water of Midian, he found a crowd of people watering their flocks, and apart from them, two women keeping back their sheep. The women explained that they could not water their flocks until the shepherds had watered theirs and withdrawn, and that their father was an elderly man. Moses watered their flocks for them, then withdrew to the shade and said, 'My Lord! Truly, I am in need of whatever good You bestow upon me.' One of the women came walking shyly and said that her father was calling him. Shu'ayb (peace be upon him) consoled Moses, saying, 'Do not fear; you have escaped from that wrongdoing people.' One of his daughters suggested hiring Moses, stating that he was strong and trustworthy. Shu'ayb offered to marry one of his two daughters to him on the condition that he work for eight years, adding that if he completed ten years, it would be of his own accord. Moses accepted this offer.\n\nWhen Moses completed the term and set out with his family, he saw a fire in the direction of Mount Tur and said, 'Wait here; perhaps I can bring you some news or a burning brand from the fire.' When he reached it, a voice called out to him from a tree on the right bank of the blessed valley: 'O Moses! Know that I am Allah, the Lord of all the worlds.' He was commanded to cast down his staff, and when it turned into a serpent, he turned away in fear and fled. Allah said, 'Draw near and do not fear.' He was instructed to place his hand into his garment so that it would come out shining white, and to draw his arms close to himself against fear. These two miracles were to be proofs against Pharaoh and his chiefs. Moses requested assistance, stating that he feared because he had killed a man previously and that his brother Aaron (Harun) was more eloquent in speech. Allah declared that He would strengthen him with his brother and that they would prevail.\n\nWhen Moses came with clear signs, they said, 'This is nothing but invented magic; we never heard of such a thing among our forefathers.' Moses said that his Lord knows best who brings guidance from Him and who will have the best outcome in the end. Pharaoh said to his nobles, 'I know of no god for you other than myself,' and ordered Haman to fire bricks out of clay and build a tower for him. He and his soldiers grew arrogant, thinking they would not be returned to Us. So We seized them and threw them into the sea. We made them leaders who invite to the Fire; on the Day of Resurrection they will not be helped, and in this world We pursued them with a curse.\n\nAfter destroying the earlier generations, Allah gave Moses the Torah. It was given as clear proofs for people, a guide, and a mercy. O Messenger! You were not on the western side when We revealed the command to Moses. Rather, We brought forth many generations; nor were you dwelling among the people of Midian, reciting Our verses to them. Nor were you beside Mount Tur when We called out. We made these known as a mercy from your Lord, so that you may warn a people to whom no warner had come before you. They used to wish that a prophet would be sent to them, but when the truth came, they said, 'Why has he not been given the like of what was given to Moses?' Yet had they not previously disbelieved in what was given to Moses? Say: 'Bring a book from Allah that is more guiding than these two, so that I may follow it.' If they do not respond to you, then know that they are only following their desires. And who could be more astray than one who follows his desires? Indeed, Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people.\n\nThat the Torah was given to mankind as guidance and mercy after the destruction of the earlier generations was so that they might reflect and take heed. Following this explanation, the wisdom behind the revelation of the Quran and the prophethood of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) are described.\n\nWhen revelation was given to Moses, you were not on the western side, by Mount Tur; nor were you among the witnesses. But We brought forth many generations, a long time passed over them, their hearts hardened, and their religions became corrupted. For this reason, it became necessary to send a new prophet and book. This is a foundational basis for the legal maxim (qawa'id al-fiqh) that 'rulings change with the change of times.' The hadith stating that someone will be sent every hundred years to renew the religion also supports this verse. However, renewal does not mean introducing innovation (bid'ah) that has no basis in the religion; every innovation is misguidance (dalalah).\n\nYou were not beside Mount Tur, but you were sent as a mercy from your Lord, to warn a people to whom no warner had come before. This people was the Arabs; although earlier prophets had come to them, no prophet was sent during the intervening generations. When the Quran came to them, they said, 'If only we had been given the like of what was given to Moses,' and regarded both the Torah and the Quran as magic. Say: Bring a book from Allah that is more guiding than these two, if you are truthful. If they cannot bring it, know that they are following their vain desires. Who can be more astray than one who follows his desires? Allah does not guide the wrongdoers.\n\nBy oath, We have conveyed the word (revelation) to them continuously so that they might reflect and take heed. Those who were given the Scripture before (those among the Jews and Christians who believed) also believe in the Quran. They will be given their reward twice; they repel evil with good, and when they hear idle talk, they turn away from it and say, 'To us our deeds, and to you your deeds.' This is a farewell greeting.\n\nYou cannot guide whom you love, but Allah guides whom He wills. This verse was revealed concerning Abu Talib. The polytheists said, \"If we follow the guidance with you, we will be torn away from our land.\" Did Allah not establish for them a secure sanctuary (Haram), to which the fruits of all things are brought? Yet most of them do not know. We have destroyed many a city that grew insolent in its prosperity. Your Lord would not destroy cities without sending a messenger. Whatever worldly goods you have been given are temporary; that which is with Allah is better and more enduring.\n\nIs he to whom a good promise was made and who attains it like one who merely enjoyed the pleasures of the worldly life and then was subjected to punishment? On that Day, Allah will call to the polytheists: \"Where are My partners?\" Their leaders will say, \"We led them astray; we dissociate ourselves before You.\" The partners will be called upon, but they will not answer. Those who repent and believe will attain salvation. Your Lord creates whatever He wills and chooses; they have no choice. Allah also knows what they conceal. The night and the day are from His mercy. On that Day, a witness will be brought forth from every nation, and their proof will be demanded; it is then understood that the truth belongs to Allah.\n\nQarun (Korah) was from the people of Moses (Musa) and transgressed against them. Allah had bestowed upon him such treasures that a company of strong men could scarcely bear their keys. His people warned him against arrogance: \"Seek the home of the Hereafter through what Allah has given you, and do not forget your share of the world. Do good as Allah has been good to you, and do not cause corruption.\" Qarun claimed that his wealth was due to the knowledge he possessed. Did he not know that Allah had destroyed generations before him? In the end, We caused the earth to swallow Qarun and his dwelling; there was no one to help him. Those who had previously desired to be in his place realized that Allah grants provision to whom He wills.\n\nThe home of the Hereafter (Paradise) is for those who do not desire haughtiness or corruption on earth. Whoever brings good will be met with better than it; whoever brings evil will only be recompensed according to what they did. Allah, who has ordained the Quran upon you, will surely return you to the place of return (Mecca). You did not expect that this Book would be revealed to you; this is a mercy from your Lord. Never support the disbelievers, and do not let them turn you away from the verses of Allah. Invite to your Lord, and do not be of the polytheists. Do not invoke any other deity alongside Allah; there is no deity but Him. Everything will perish except His Essence; the judgment belongs to Him, and to Him you will be returned.\n\nIn this verse, the concept of the Face of Allah (wajh) is explained. There is no deity but Him, and everything will perish except His Face. That is to say, every entity other than the Essence of Allah is in reality deemed non-existent, because their existence does not originate from themselves, but depends upon Allah.\n\nSince created beings are receptive to perishing at every moment, they are in truth as if non-existent, or they will perish one day. Yet Allah is Ever-Living in His Essence, eternal and everlasting; His existence is self-subsistent. This is the meaning preferred by the majority.\n\nAccording to another interpretation, 'face' (wajh) means the intended direction. In this case, the verse conveys that everything is in destruction except the direction intended for Allah's pleasure and approval—that is, even the blessings of the Hereafter are not temporary.\n\nA third interpretation is as follows: Everything has a face turned toward Allah, which is its true form in Allah's knowledge. The return of everything through this face is to Allah. That is, every being returns to Allah, who is the purpose of its creation.\n\nJudgment belongs solely to Allah; the judgment of anyone else is invalid. The rule of those who attempt to legislate will be annulled, and only Allah's judgment will endure. In the end, all of you will be returned to Him and held to account. The conclusion of all the narratives is precisely this decree of return. No one can fail to submit to this judgment."